@page "/docs/architecture/data-access-browser"

@* Data access in the browser: the client store over HTTP that WebAssembly and Auto pages ask, the endpoints
   behind it, registering a store in both projects for prerendering, and the checks that move to the endpoint.
   A grid's request over HTTP is in Common/DataApi.razor. *@

<ArticlePage Section="architecture" Slug="data-access-browser">
    <Lead>
        WebAssembly and Auto pages run in the browser, which has no connection to the database. The page stays the
        same, and a second store calls your API for it.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">The client store and its endpoints</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The page still asks <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access">the interface</Anchor>. A second store, in
            the <Code>.Client</Code> project, calls an API, and the API's endpoints use
            <Anchor Href="docs/architecture/data-access-server">the server's store</Anchor>:
        </TextBlock>
        <figure class="site-diagram">
            <div class="site-diagram-scroll ib-scroll">@((MarkupString)InBrowser)</div>
            <figcaption>WebAssembly and Auto: the page runs in the browser, and its store calls the server.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="HttpCustomerStore.cs (client)">@HttpStore</Code>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs (server)">@Endpoints</Code>
        <Heading Level="2">Both projects need a store</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Share the interface and the row types.</strong> Put <Code>ICustomerStore</Code> and
                <Code>CustomerRow</Code> in the <Code>.Client</Code> project, which the server project references. Return a
                type made for the API, not an entity with columns the user shouldn't see.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Register a store in both projects.</strong> The page prerenders on the server, and an Auto page
                runs there until the WebAssembly runtime has downloaded. So the server registers
                <Code>EfCustomerStore</Code> and the client registers <Code>HttpCustomerStore</Code>. With only the
                client's, prerendering fails. See Microsoft's
                <Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/components/prerender" External>Prerender components</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Carry the first load over.</strong> The page loads its data once while prerendering and again
                when it starts in the browser. To skip the second load, persist what the first one read. See
                <Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/state-management/prerendered-state-persistence" External>Prerendered state persistence</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Registering the stores">@Registration</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A Table or DataGrid sends its sort, filters and page with each load. To send that request over HTTP, see
            <Anchor Href="docs/common/data-api">Data from an API</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Checks that move to the server</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A user can change code that runs in the browser, and can call your endpoints without your page. So an
            endpoint repeats every check that the page made:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Who can call it.</strong> Give the endpoint the policy that the page's <Code>[Authorize]</Code>
                names, with <Code>RequireAuthorization</Code>. See Microsoft's
                <Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/security/" External>Blazor authentication and authorization</Anchor>.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>What it accepts.</strong> Validate a posted model again in the endpoint. The form's validation in
                the browser only helps the user.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Which rows it returns.</strong> If users see only their own records, the store filters by the
                signed-in user on the server. Don't take the user's id from the request.
            </ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/data-api">Data from an API</Anchor>: a grid's sort, filters and paging over HTTP, and what the server limits.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/call-web-api" External>Call a web API from Blazor</Anchor>: Microsoft's guide to <Code>HttpClient</Code>, named clients and errors.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string HttpStore = """
        public sealed class HttpCustomerStore(HttpClient api) : ICustomerStore
        {
            public async Task<IReadOnlyList<CustomerRow>> ListAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default) =>
                await api.GetFromJsonAsync<List<CustomerRow>>("api/customers", cancellationToken) ?? [];

            public async Task<CustomerRow?> GetAsync(int id, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
            {
                using var response = await api.GetAsync($"api/customers/{id}", cancellationToken);
                if (response.StatusCode == HttpStatusCode.NotFound)
                {
                    return null;
                }
                response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
                return await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<CustomerRow>(cancellationToken);
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string Endpoints = """
        var customers = app.MapGroup("/api/customers").RequireAuthorization("Sales");

        customers.MapGet("/", (ICustomerStore store, CancellationToken cancellationToken) =>
            store.ListAsync(cancellationToken));

        customers.MapGet("/{id:int}", async (int id, ICustomerStore store, CancellationToken cancellationToken) =>
            await store.GetAsync(id, cancellationToken) is { } customer ? Results.Ok(customer) : Results.NotFound());
        """;

    private const string Registration = """
        // Program.cs (server)
        builder.Services.AddDbContextFactory<ShopDb>(options => options.UseSqlite(connectionString));
        builder.Services.AddScoped<ICustomerStore, EfCustomerStore>();

        // Program.cs (client)
        builder.Services.AddScoped(_ => new HttpClient { BaseAddress = new Uri(builder.HostEnvironment.BaseAddress) });
        builder.Services.AddScoped<ICustomerStore, HttpCustomerStore>();
        """;

